INT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - BOOTH - DAY

A cramped booth. A fan that's seen better decades. A gate arm. A vending machine that only sells one flavour.

A GUARD (50s, belly, clipboard, tired eyes) sits and eats a sandwich. A sign behind him reads: NEXT WINDOW PLEASE.

A beat-up van pulls up outside. Its side reads: THE GENTLE BASTARDS - TOUR 2025.

THE BAND (four people, leather jackets, instruments) climbs out in a line and walks up to the window.

GUARD
Passports.

THE BAND
(in unison, ragged)
Hi!

APPLAUSE.

GUARD
(not looking up)
That's cute. Passports.

THE BAND
(handing them over in a stack)
Here.

He flips through. Stops.

GUARD
You're all Gary.

THE BAND
(shrugging)
It's a stage name.

GUARD
It's the same name on four passports.

THE BAND
(proudly)
We're consistent.

LAUGHS.

The Guard sighs, puts down his sandwich and picks up his phone. A text lights up the screen.

ON PHONE: dad where r u
ON PHONE: ur shift ends in 10

GUARD
(typing)
Busy, Mel. Border stuff.

He puts it down. Looks at the band again. A pause. Too long.

GUARD (CONT'D)
Where's the drummer?

THE BAND
(looking at each other)
What drummer?

GUARD
The one in the van.

Beat. The band turns slowly. In the van window, a tiny dog in sunglasses stares back at them. WOOF.

APPLAUSE.

GUARD (CONT'D)
(standing, finally)
That's not a drummer. That's a dog.

THE BAND
(quietly, to each other)
He's a very good dog.

The Guard reads the name on his badge, taps it. WALT.

WALT
Call me Walt. And your dog goes in the quarantine bay.

THE BAND
(in unison)
We've never been so insulted.

Walt hits a button. The gate arm drops. The fan whirs.

WALT (CONT'D)
(softly)
That's two hours, folks. Enjoy the vending machine.

A beat. Then the vending machine, from nowhere, drops a can of soup. THUNK.

LAUGHS. APPLAUSE. CUT TO:
